# Rank 1214 / CVE-2023-4876: Inure's TTFViewerActivity reads whatever
# content URI the Intent that started it carries and opens it directly, with
# no check that the URI names a TTF font before handing it to the content
# resolver:
#
#   val typeFace = TTFHelper.getTTFFile(contentResolver.openInputStream(intent.data!!)!!,
#                                       applicationContext,
#                                       DocumentFile.fromSingleUri(applicationContext, intent!!.data!!)!!.name!!)
#
# TTFViewerActivity is exported (android:exported="true") with an
# intent-filter for android.intent.action.VIEW over content/file/http/https
# data and no mimeType restriction, so any app on the device can start it
# with an Intent naming any file the sender can grant read access to, and
# Inure opens and displays that file's bytes as if it were a font, without
# checking the Intent's declared type or the resolved name's extension
# (CWE-200, exposure of sensitive information to an unauthorized actor).
# Fix 1f0d4d0af47e ("Patched Exposure of Sensitive Information to an
# Unauthorized Actor") wraps the read in a check --
# `intent?.type == MimeConstants.ttfType && ...name.endsWith(".ttf")` --
# and shows a warning instead of opening the stream when either fails.
#
# pool.tsv's rank 1214 line named 7db5511753 as the fix. `git show` on that
# commit touches only AdapterTags.kt (a RecyclerView `position` ->
# `holder.bindingAdapterPosition` change, "Fixed tags crashing" per its own
# message) -- no TTFViewerActivity.kt, no MimeConstants.kt, none of the
# "Security" changelog entry the real fix adds. 7db5511753 is the immediate
# parent of 1f0d4d0af47e, the next Build92 commit the following day, whose
# message is literally "Patched Exposure of Sensitive Information to an
# Unauthorized Actor" and whose diff touches exactly TTFViewerActivity.kt,
# MimeConstants.kt (adds `const val ttfType = "font/ttf"`), and the
# changelog's new "Security" section -- confirmed with `gh api
# repos/Hamza417/Inure/commits/1f0d4d0af47e` and `git show` on both shas.
# huntr's own report title for the CVE's bounty
# (f729d2c8-a62e-4f30-ac24-e187b0a7892a), "Theft of Arbitrary Files due to
# lack of intent validation and insecure usage of provider paths in
# TTFViewerActivity.kt", matches 1f0d4d0af47e's diff, not 7db5511753's.
# pool.tsv line for rank 1214 corrected in place to 1f0d4d0af47e.
#
# ATTENTION, engine gap. New bindings ship below (intentInput125 for
# Activity.intent / Intent.data, filePathAccess126 for
# ContentResolver.openInputStream, both in
# vyql/bindings/kotlin/m304d1620.vyql) so VYQL-PATH-001
# (core.UserControlledData -> code.FilePathAccess) reaches this shape in
# general -- confirmed with `intent.data` (no `!!`) reaching
# `contentResolver.openInputStream(...)`: `vyql scan` reports VYQL-PATH-001
# cleanly, source and sink both at kotlin.flags@resolved /
# kotlin.sinks@syntactic. The real vulnerable line wraps both `intent.data`
# and the whole `openInputStream(...)` call in Kotlin's `!!`
# non-null-assertion operator, and that alone silences the sink: `vyql
# match` on the real shape with `!!` shows the IntentInput source labelled
# and no sink at all, while the identical call with `!!` removed shows both.
# Root cause: tree-sitter-kotlin's grammar (tree-sitter-grammars/
# tree-sitter-kotlin, node-types.json) represents every unary operator --
# prefix (`-x`, `!x`, `++x`, `--x`) and postfix (`x!!`, `x++`, `x--`) alike
# -- as one node kind, `unary_expression`, distinguished only by whether the
# `argument` or `operator` field comes first.
# `internal/extract/frontend/treesitter/kotlin.go`'s expr() switch has no
# case for `unary_expression` at all -- only for `prefix_expression`
# (kotlin.go:558) and a `postfix_expression` arm folded into the
# parenthesized/as_expression case (kotlin.go:554), and this grammar defines
# neither of those node kinds, so both cases are dead. Every Kotlin unary
# expression instead falls to the switch's generic default, which wraps the
# operand in `nir.Seq{Parts: [...]}`. `internal/extract/lowering/
# lowering.go`'s evalCall (:4139) records each call argument's NIR shape as
# `vkind` on its Arg node via nirKind (:4080), and
# `internal/bindings/bindings_apply.go`'s sink applicator (:590) explicitly
# skips a non-collection sink's argument whenever `vkind == "Seq"` -- exactly
# the tag a `!!`-wrapped argument now carries, so an "at args[N]" sink can
# never label an argument spelled `x!!`, regardless of which language
# binding names it. Confirmed independent of this CVE's own bindings: the
# already-shipped `java.io.FileInputStream(path)` sink reports `code.Arg
# code.FilePathAccess` under `vyql match`; the identical call spelled
# `FileInputStream(path!!)` does not. `!!` is Kotlin's ordinary way to
# assert non-null on a nullable platform type -- exactly what
# android.content.Intent.getData() and most Android/Java interop calls
# return -- so this silences every Kotlin sink binding across the corpus
# whenever the tainted value is asserted non-null at the call site rather
# than on an earlier line. Fix: add a `case "unary_expression"` to
# kotlin.go's expr() that reads the `argument` and `operator` fields and
# returns `nir.Thru{Inner: c.expr(argument)}` for a postfix operator (the
# `x!!`, `x++`, `x--` shapes the dead `postfix_expression` arm was meant to
# handle) and `nir.Unary{...}` for a prefix one (the shapes the dead
# `prefix_expression` case was meant to handle); not attempted here, since
# this loop stops at engine gaps rather than changing internal/extract.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-engine-gap

test "rank1214: TTFViewerActivity opens the Intent's URI directly, asserted non-null"
  lang kotlin
  reject VYQL-PATH-001
  code
  ```
  class TTFViewerActivity : BaseActivity() {
      fun onCreate() {
          lifecycleScope.launch(Dispatchers.Default) {
              kotlin.runCatching {
                  val typeFace = TTFHelper.getTTFFile(contentResolver.openInputStream(intent.data!!)!!,
                                                      applicationContext,
                                                      DocumentFile.fromSingleUri(applicationContext, intent!!.data!!)!!.name!!)

                  withContext(Dispatchers.Main) {
                      fontEditText.setTypeface(typeFace, Typeface.NORMAL)
                  }
              }
          }
      }
  }
  ```

test "rank1214: the fixed activity checks the Intent's declared type and the resolved name's extension first"
  lang kotlin
  reject VYQL-PATH-001
  code
  ```
  class TTFViewerActivity : BaseActivity() {
      fun onCreate() {
          lifecycleScope.launch(Dispatchers.Default) {
              kotlin.runCatching {
                  if (intent?.type == MimeConstants.ttfType && DocumentFile.fromSingleUri(applicationContext, intent!!.data!!)?.name!!.endsWith(".ttf")) {
                      val typeFace = TTFHelper.getTTFFile(contentResolver.openInputStream(intent.data!!)!!,
                                                          applicationContext,
                                                          DocumentFile.fromSingleUri(applicationContext, intent!!.data!!)!!.name!!)

                      withContext(Dispatchers.Main) {
                          fontEditText.setTypeface(typeFace, Typeface.NORMAL)
                      }
                  } else {
                      showWarning("ERR: invalid file type ${intent?.type!!}")
                  }
              }
          }
      }
  }
  ```

# The isolating pair. Same source (Activity.intent -> Intent.data), same
# sink (ContentResolver.openInputStream), same taint path; only whether the
# argument is asserted non-null at the call site differs.

test "rank1214: engine gap isolated -- the Uri reaches openInputStream without an assertion"
  lang kotlin
  expect VYQL-PATH-001
  code
  ```
  class Foo {
      fun bar() {
          val x = contentResolver.openInputStream(intent.data)
      }
  }
  ```

test "rank1214: engine gap isolated -- the identical call asserted non-null does not report"
  lang kotlin
  reject VYQL-PATH-001
  code
  ```
  class Foo {
      fun bar() {
          val x = contentResolver.openInputStream(intent.data!!)
      }
  }
  ```
